; repro-teardown.run — reproduce the Dell Software Failure on riqemu1.
;
; THE DEFECT (dca6bda's commit message has the long version):
; `ri_pal_midi_close()` stopped the BRIDGE task and deleted the camd link
; WITHOUT stopping the sender task named `RIAPP midi-out`. That task keeps its
; 1 ms loop, keeps calling `ri_pal_midi_send`, keeps reading `s_txlink`, and
; keeps calling `PutMidi` -- on a link being freed underneath it. The Dell said:
;
;     RIAPP midi out   Error: illegal address access
;                     exec_777_sendio
;
; WHY `Break ... C` IS THE TRIGGER AND NOT A CONVENIENCE.
; The app's documented quit is the close gadget or `Break <cli> C`, and BOTH
; run the same teardown ending in `ri_pal_midi_close()`. So `Break` is not a
; shortcut around the GUI -- it is the documented path, and it is the one that
; can be driven from a script. That is what makes this reproducible at all:
; a crash that needs a human to click a gadget is a crash that only reproduces
; on the machine the human is sitting at.
;
; THE STRESSER IS THE PLAYING SONG. With clock out on, the render produces F8
; ticks and the sender is inside `PutMidi` on a loop. Quitting into that is
; the race. A quit with nothing sending would be a quieter test that might
; pass by luck, which is exactly how the bug survived: it is timing, and a
; test that does not create the timing teaches you nothing.
;
; RUN IT AGAINST BOTH BINARIES. Prefix (b10e2b7) crashes; dca6bda does not.
; One without the other proves nothing -- a test that passes on the fixed
; build only says the fixed build passed.

setenv RIAPP_EVLOG RAM:
setenv RIAPP_LOG RAM:
setenv RIAPP_DEMO 0
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_CLKOUT 1
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_DEVOUT 1
setenv RIAPP_MIDI_LATMS 8

Run >NIL: RAM:RIAPP
Wait 10
Break RIAPP C
Wait 6
type RAM:RIAPP.LOG
